{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to a20d9353: confirmed. The existing-identity path calls no\n`unadmit`.\n\n1. When `admit` succeeds and `build` or the signing then fails, the\n   earlier record keeps its raw bytes, and the reservation of that\n   identity stays.\n2. The reason, from your inventory f8f35048: `arm` tops up one identity\n   to a fixed need and adds nothing when the reservation already covers\n   it. A repeated failed attempt therefore holds no more than one\n   successful attempt holds. The count of identities keeps its own\n   limit.\n3. A later result write or terminal write releases the reservation, as\n   it does today.\n4. The C3 note states items 1 to 3 with the source lines of `arm` and\n   of the release. It also states that `unadmit` stays a new-identity\n   operation.\n5. One witness stays enough: the refusing store of 6ad2e7bc item 3. Add\n   no test of the build failure after a successful `admit`. The\n   existing SQLite owner covers the `arm` accounting.\n\nA store other than SQLite follows the same rule: its `admit` for an\nexisting identity is safe to repeat. Write that sentence into the\ncomment of the `admit` port.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a20d93532884d6033ca66be5658f344752989c07 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:003802bea882e6c5c61885187bad460807f9b07f, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01af183f1fd462ece996760281d760bfe03a427c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0494d214d1779ad41e6408903f743dc98d35c57c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04e73625fd8bfed4b211546238ef7a71b1ad34f9 and 170 more","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}